1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,569
…Two workers walk out of the same Samsung plant in Korea.

2
00:00:06,569 --> 00:00:10,632
Same shift, they worked it a hundred yards apart.

3
00:00:10,632 --> 00:00:16,195
One of them builds memory chips. This year, his bonus runs toward four hundred thousand

4
00:00:16,195 --> 00:00:21,594
dollars… The other builds phones and televisions.

5
00:00:21,594 --> 00:00:24,634
His bonus comes to about four thousand.

6
00:00:24,634 --> 00:00:31,620
Same company, same shift, a hundred to one … That

7
00:00:31,620 --> 00:00:34,880
gap is the whole AI boom in one picture.

8
00:00:34,880 --> 00:00:38,530
One boom hit two different bottlenecks. Two places

9
00:00:38,530 --> 00:00:42,382
where everything backs up because one thing is in short supply.

10
00:00:42,382 --> 00:00:45,715
At the first one, the workers walked off with a fortune.

11
00:00:45,715 --> 00:00:49,875
At the second one, the cost landed on people who never got a vote.

12
00:00:49,875 --> 00:00:56,505
Same boom, opposite endings, and one question decides which end you land on.

13
00:00:56,505 --> 00:01:04,754
Who gets to say no … So why did the chip worker win?

14
00:01:04,754 --> 00:01:08,054
Because he was sitting on the one part the whole boom can't run without

15
00:01:08,054 --> 00:01:13,152
, the fast memory that stacks inside every AI chip.

16
00:01:13,152 --> 00:01:17,952
Right now, it's sold out, and Samsung is not a normal company.

17
00:01:17,952 --> 00:01:21,737
It's almost a quarter of South Korea's entire exports.

18
00:01:21,737 --> 00:01:26,027
So one walkout in that plant doesn't dent a single company's quarter.

19
00:01:26,027 --> 00:01:29,647
It stalls the AI build out for the whole planet.

20
00:01:29,647 --> 00:01:33,247
Their profit had jumped seven hundred and fifty five percent in a year

21
00:01:33,247 --> 00:01:38,044
, so the workers asked for a cut, and they got it.

22
00:01:38,044 --> 00:01:42,882
Ten and a half percent of the division's profit…every year for ten years

23
00:01:42,882 --> 00:01:47,256
…

24
00:01:47,256 --> 00:01:51,336
And this is the part that shows up in every other story like it.

25
00:01:51,336 --> 00:01:54,776
The chip workers had two things, a union

26
00:01:54,776 --> 00:01:58,891
…and a product nobody could buy anywhere else.

27
00:01:58,891 --> 00:02:02,291
Put those together and they could shut the whole line down.

28
00:02:02,291 --> 00:02:05,893
So when they asked for a cut, the answer had to be yes.

29
00:02:05,893 --> 00:02:10,816
That's what this comes down to, the power to say no and make it stick.

30
00:02:10,816 --> 00:02:15,546
The bonus is just the receipt. So hold on to the question.

31
00:02:15,546 --> 00:02:20,377
Who gets to say no… Because the rest of this boom is full of people who can't

32
00:02:20,377 --> 00:02:24,575
…

33
00:02:24,575 --> 00:02:27,915
Now follow the electricity instead of the chips.

34
00:02:27,915 --> 00:02:30,942
It leads straight to the second bottleneck.

35
00:02:30,942 --> 00:02:35,530
Every data center needs a giant transformer before it can switch on.

36
00:02:35,530 --> 00:02:39,010
That's the equipment that moves power across the grid.

37
00:02:39,010 --> 00:02:44,042
The wait for a big one has stretched to four years, and it gets stranger.

38
00:02:44,042 --> 00:02:47,222
The special steel inside that transformer's core comes from

39
00:02:47,222 --> 00:02:51,055
exactly one factory in the entire United States.

40
00:02:51,055 --> 00:02:56,907
One mill in Butler, Pennsylvania. The CEO of that company says it plainly.

41
00:02:56,907 --> 00:03:02,490
No AI without electricity…and no electricity without transformers.

42
00:03:02,490 --> 00:03:07,100
The grid is a choke point exactly like the memory plant with one difference

43
00:03:07,100 --> 00:03:10,877
…

44
00:03:10,877 --> 00:03:14,535
The difference is that nobody here has a hand on the valve.

45
00:03:14,535 --> 00:03:19,312
The grid operator for thirteen eastern states ran its auctions for future power.

46
00:03:19,312 --> 00:03:22,602
The math came back ugly. Data center demand

47
00:03:22,602 --> 00:03:26,462
added almost fourteen billion dollars to customer bills.

48
00:03:26,462 --> 00:03:31,432
wholesale power up more than seventy five percent in a single year.

49
00:03:31,432 --> 00:03:34,852
That lands on families in Ohio and Virginia.

50
00:03:34,852 --> 00:03:38,172
They never voted on the data centers driving the number.

51
00:03:38,172 --> 00:03:43,387
They just pay more for the same electricity. The chip workers had a union.

52
00:03:43,387 --> 00:03:46,057
The person opening that power bill has a billing address

53
00:03:46,057 --> 00:03:50,503
…

54
00:03:50,503 --> 00:03:53,873
Here's where the easy version of this story falls apart.

55
00:03:53,873 --> 00:03:57,803
You'd think the chip workers bonus just gets passed down to you.

56
00:03:57,803 --> 00:04:01,203
Higher chip prices, higher everything, your bill goes up.

57
00:04:01,203 --> 00:04:04,751
Clean and simple. It doesn't work that way.

58
00:04:04,751 --> 00:04:08,898
The bonus is a slice of profit. It comes out of what's left after the

59
00:04:08,898 --> 00:04:13,101
chips sell, not out of the price stamped on each one.

60
00:04:13,101 --> 00:04:18,693
So his raise did not pay for your bill, and your bill did not fund his raise.

61
00:04:18,693 --> 00:04:22,193
They come out of the same boom. They are not the same money

62
00:04:22,193 --> 00:04:27,638
… And this is already spreading.

63
00:04:27,638 --> 00:04:34,268
Within days of the Samsung vote, TSMC, the one other chipmaker with the same kind of pull,

64
00:04:34,268 --> 00:04:38,713
told its own staff their profit sharing would jump more than thirty percent.

65
00:04:38,713 --> 00:04:44,763
In the US, two of the biggest unions are studying exactly what Samsung's workers just pulled off.

66
00:04:44,763 --> 00:04:49,891
There's talk of an AI dividend showing up at company shareholder meetings next year.

67
00:04:49,891 --> 00:04:56,321
So the same split travels with it. Where workers can organize, they write themselves a claim on the boom.

68
00:04:56,321 --> 00:05:05,599
Where they can't, the bill just shows up in the mailbox … The four hundred thousand dollar bonus is the headline.

69
00:05:05,599 --> 00:05:12,322
The thing I actually feel is a line on my own power bill, and I don't live anywhere near a data center.

70
00:05:12,322 --> 00:05:15,879
you don't have to live near one for the boom to reach the bill.

71
00:05:15,879 --> 00:05:20,747
I'm not the four hundred thousand dollar worker here. Odds are you aren't either.

72
00:05:20,747 --> 00:05:24,327
So picture those two workers walking out of the plant again.

73
00:05:24,327 --> 00:05:27,377
Even the four thousand dollar guy had a union and a vote.

74
00:05:27,377 --> 00:05:33,332
He was still inside the deal. The person opening that power bill was never inside it.

75
00:05:33,332 --> 00:05:37,072
The chip workers had a hand on the valve, and they turned it.

76
00:05:37,072 --> 00:05:45,511
The rest of us are still standing somewhere with no valve to grab … I kept the biggest pieces for the article.

77
00:05:45,511 --> 00:05:51,043
A one paragraph deal from nineteen fifty one that this contract quietly rhymes with.

78
00:05:51,043 --> 00:05:57,915
Korea's plan to mail every citizen a check funded by AI tax money, the fracture inside Samsung

79
00:05:57,915 --> 00:06:01,195
where the phone and TV workers backed this at twenty one percent

80
00:06:01,195 --> 00:06:05,796
, the shareholders now suing to tear the whole deal up.

81
00:06:05,796 --> 00:06:11,536
and the question I can't close, does Samsung make the cloud giants pay for it?

82
00:06:11,536 --> 00:06:17,606
It's all in the piece. … The full piece is on substack.

83
00:06:17,606 --> 00:06:22,443
The two workers, the power bill, and the one question that decides who wins this boom.

84
00:06:22,443 --> 00:06:28,201
The civic note. Subscribe free at drink your o j dot substack dot com.

85
00:06:28,201 --> 00:06:31,581
New issues every week…
